 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No, not yet.** Inflation is running well above the Reserve Bank's 2–3% target band, and it has been rising again since mid-2025.\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026, up from 1.9% in June 2025. It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and eased to 3.5% in July before rising again.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026. It has been above 3% since October 2025 and has risen steadily from 2.8% in June 2025.\n\nThe RBA has responded by raising the cash rate from 3.6% in January 2026 to 4.36% in September 2026. The trimmed mean has risen for most of the past year despite those increases, so the policy response is still working through the economy.\n\nInflation is well off its 2023 peak of about 7%, but it is not back in the target range and is moving in the wrong direction. The September quarter CPI, due at the end of October, will be the next key read.",
